## Interfaces in Go

Interfaces define behavior without implementing it. Types "implement" an interface by providing its methods.

### Defining an Interface

```go
type Shape interface {
    Area() float64
    Perimeter() float64
}
```

### Implementing an Interface

```go
type Rectangle struct {
    Width, Height float64
}

// This makes Rectangle implement Shape
func (r Rectangle) Area() float64 {
    return r.Width * r.Height
}

func (r Rectangle) Perimeter() float64 {
    return 2 * (r.Width + r.Height)
}
```

### Using Interfaces

```go
rect := Rectangle{Width: 3, Height: 4}
var s Shape = rect
fmt.Println(s.Area()) // 12
```

### Empty Interface

```go
var anything interface{} // can hold any value
anything = 42
anything = "hello"
anything = []int{1, 2, 3}
```

### Type Assertions

```go
var s Shape = Rectangle{3, 4}

// Check if s is actually a Rectangle
rect, ok := s.(Rectangle)
if ok {
    fmt.Println(rect.Width)
}
```

### Type Switches

```go
func describe(i interface{}) {
    switch v := i.(type) {
    case int:
        fmt.Println("int:", v)
    case string:
        fmt.Println("string:", v)
    default:
        fmt.Println("unknown")
    }
}
```

### Interface is Contract

- Methods must have the exact signatures
- No explicit "implements" keyword - implicit implementation
- Interfaces should be small and focused

### Common Interface Patterns


```go
// Reader interface (from io package)
type Reader interface {
    Read(p []byte) (n int, err error)
}

// Writer interface
type Writer interface {
    Write(p []byte) (n int, err error)
}

// Error interface
type Error interface {
    Error() string
}
```

